// The MATRIX family — the last of the core words, and the smallest word
// surface in the language.
//
// There is no matrix literal and no constructor word. The reference's whole
// matrix surface is two conversions and two aliases onto them:
//
//   convert.to_matrix    convert.to_matrix.
//   matrix               matrix.
//
// registered at `reference/rust_multistackvm/src/stdlib/convert/internal.rs:120-121`
// and aliased at `create_aliases.rs:43-44`. So a matrix is only ever something
// a LIST of LISTs is converted into, and this probe is that conversion in
// every shape a program can write it.
//
// **A matrix carries rows of its own** — `Payload::Matrix`, the reference's
// shape (D57 as amended). An earlier design tagged a LIST instead, which is
// invisible from Bund but not from a golden: the oracle renders
// `data: Matrix([[…]])` with bare rows, where a tagged list renders
// `data: List([Value { dt: 9, … }])` — a different payload name, one more
// level, and a header per row. `render` is what a golden captures, so the
// representation is observable and has to be the reference's.
//
// Not covered here, deliberately:
//   convert.to_dict / convert.to_dict. — the reference's word converts to a
//   MATRIX, not a dict (F120: both bodies pass MATRIX while their prefixes say
//   CONVERT.TO_DICT). Bund2 corrects that to a MAP per D57, so a golden
//   captured from the oracle would disagree by design. F120 is the record.

// --- convert.to_matrix: a LIST of LISTs becomes a MATRIX ---
// The rows survive as LIST values; only the outer tag moves, to 26.
[ [ 1 2 ] [ 3 4 ] ] convert.to_matrix

// --- the `matrix` alias reaches the same word ---
[ [ 5 6 ] [ 7 8 ] ] matrix

// --- a single row, and an empty list ---
// An empty LIST converts to an empty MATRIX rather than failing: the row loop
// simply has nothing to run (`conv.rs:361-378`).
[ [ 9 ] ] matrix
list matrix

// --- a row that is not a LIST is wrapped, not refused ---
// Each row goes through `conv(LIST)` before it is cast, and an INTEGER
// converts to a one-element LIST (`conv.rs:82`), so `[ 1 2 ]` is a matrix of
// two one-cell rows rather than an error. That is the arm a reader expects to
// fail and which does not.
[ 1 2 ] matrix

// --- floats, as the oracle's own round-trip test uses ---
// `reference/rust_dynamic/tests/conv-test.rs:164-173` converts exactly this
// shape and reads the cell back.
[ [ 42.0 41.0 ] ] matrix

// --- back to a LIST: a MATRIX converts to a LIST of its rows ---
// `value_matrix_conversion` (`conv.rs:268-290`) is the only conversion a
// MATRIX source has, so this is the whole of the return path.
[ [ 1 2 ] [ 3 4 ] ] matrix convert.to_list

// Not covered here either:
//   `matrix matrix` — converting a MATRIX to a MATRIX. The oracle refuses it:
//   `value_matrix_conversion` (`conv.rs:268-290`) accepts a LIST target and
//   nothing else, so a MATRIX source with a MATRIX target falls to its `_`
//   arm. Bund2 refuses it the same way, with the reference's text, but a
//   refusal on its own line would end the capture, so it lives in a unit test
//   rather than here.

// --- the length of a matrix ---
// `len` sums the row lengths rather than counting rows
// (`reference/rust_dynamic/src/len.rs:33-45`), so a 2x2 answers 4 and a 1x4
// answers 4 as well: the shape is not recoverable from it.
[ [ 1 2 ] [ 3 4 ] ] matrix len println
[ [ 1 2 3 4 ] ] matrix len println

// --- convert.to_matrix.: the operand comes off the workbench, and the
// --- answer goes back to it ---
// `stdlib_convert_base`'s workbench arm guards the workbench and pushes back
// there (`internal.rs:14-15,21,29`), so the value has to be `return`ed first
// and `take`n afterwards to be visible.
[ [ 1 2 ] [ 3 4 ] ] return convert.to_matrix. take

// --- the `matrix.` alias, the same way ---
[ [ 5 6 ] ] return matrix. take

// --- a workbench matrix converted back, still on the workbench ---
[ [ 7 8 ] ] return matrix. convert.to_list. take
